# [Maximize Consecutive Elements in an Array After Modification](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximize-consecutive-elements-in-an-array-after-modification/)

### Approach: Sorting + Recursion + Memoization (DP)

1. Sort the array to process elements in increasing order.
2. Use recursion with three states:
   - `curr`: Current index.
   - `prev`: Previously selected element's index.
   - `inc`: Whether the previous element was incremented by 1.
3. For every element, consider two choices:
   - Take it without incrementing.
   - Take it after incrementing by 1.
4. Use memoization with `dp[prev + 1][inc]` to avoid repeated calculations.
5. Also consider skipping the current element and maximize the resulting sequence length.

### C++ Code

### C++ Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int dp[100001][2];

    int solve(vector<int>& nums, int curr, int prev, bool inc) {
        if(curr >= nums.size()) return 0;

        if(prev != -1 && ((!inc && nums[curr] > nums[prev] + 1) ||
                          (inc && nums[prev] + 2 < nums[curr])))
            return 0;

        if(dp[prev + 1][inc] != -1)
            return dp[prev + 1][inc];

        int take = 0, not_take = 0;

        if(prev == -1) {
            take = max(1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, false),
                       1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, true));
        }
        else if(inc) {
            if(nums[prev] + 2 == nums[curr])
                take = 1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, false);

            if(nums[prev] + 2 == nums[curr] + 1)
                take = 1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, true);
        }
        else {
            if(nums[prev] + 1 == nums[curr])
                take = 1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, false);

            if(nums[prev] + 1 == nums[curr] + 1)
                take = 1 + solve(nums, curr + 1, curr, true);
        }

        not_take = solve(nums, curr + 1, prev, inc);

        return dp[prev + 1][inc] = max(take, not_take);
    }

    int maxSelectedElements(vector<int>& nums) {
        memset(dp, -1, sizeof(dp));
        sort(begin(nums), end(nums));
        return solve(nums, 0, -1, false);
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis

- Sorting: \(O(n\log n)\)
- DP storage: \(O(n)\)
- Auxiliary recursion stack: \(O(n)\)

### Key Learnings

- Sorting helps simplify transitions between consecutive elements.
- Take/Not Take recursion can represent multiple possibilities.
- Tracking whether the previous element was incremented helps determine valid transitions.
- Memoization avoids recomputing previously evaluated states.
